---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const beds = assertion('twelve-homes-and-eight-hundred-and-eleven-beds')
const chains = assertion('two-thirds-of-the-beds-are-run-from-elsewhere')
const bench = assertion('better-rated-and-no-better-staffed')
const defs = assertion('two-hundred-and-eighty-seven-deficiencies')
const star = assertion('a-five-star-home-is-the-most-dangerous-workplace')
const days = assertion('the-homes-are-a-seventh-smaller-than-in-2013')
const mcd = assertion('medicaid-pays-for-three-days-in-five')

const spec = (a: typeof beds, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const bedsSpec = spec(beds, 'Occupancy, per cent of certified beds')
const benchSpec = spec(bench, 'Mean overall five-star rating')
const starSpec = spec(star, 'Recordable injuries per 100 full-time workers')
const daysSpec = spec(days, 'Resident days in a year')
---

<Article slug="five-stars">
  <p>
    On any night about seven hundred Allen County residents sleep in a nursing home. Where they are,
    who runs the building, how it is staffed and what an inspector found there the last time are all
    public, and the county's own record of them has been empty. Two federal files close that: a
    register of every certified home in the country, and thirteen years of the financial reports
    each one files.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={beds} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="beds"
    title="How full the beds are"
    caption="Residents per day against certified beds, August 2026. Allen County's homes run fuller than the state's and considerably fuller than the country's."
    columns={['Place', 'Occupancy']}
    rows={beds.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="beds" set:html={JSON.stringify(bedsSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Twelve, not eleven. CMS's own county column finds only eleven of them, because it takes the
    county from the provider's enrollment record rather than from where the building stands, and it
    puts The Meadows of Delphos across the line in Van Wert. The Census Bureau's address ranges
    return an exact match in Allen County, and CMS's cost report for the same certification says
    Allen in every year it has filed from that address.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={chains} />
  <p>
    Three of the twelve belong to Trilogy Health Services, which runs a hundred and twenty-three
    homes; two to HCF Management, which runs twenty-two. The three that answer to nobody outside the
    county are the Lima Convalescent Home and the two Mennonite houses at Bluffton, and between them
    they hold a fifth of the beds.
  </p>

  <h2>What the inspectors found</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={defs} />
  <p>
    A citation is graded on a grid from A to L for how many residents it touched and how badly. Most
    of these — two hundred and one of the two hundred and eighty-seven — are at D, which means a
    problem that harmed nobody. The two at the top of the scale are the ones a reader should hold
    onto, and so are the seventeen citations, at seven different homes, for failures of the
    residents' freedom from abuse and neglect.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={bench} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="bench"
    title="Rated better than the state"
    caption="Mean overall five-star rating. Turnover and staffing tell a flatter story: the county is at the state median on nursing hours and below it on churn."
    columns={['Place', 'Mean rating']}
    rows={bench.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="bench" set:html={JSON.stringify(benchSpec)} is:inline />

  <h2>What the stars do not cover</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={star} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="star"
    title="One home against the county"
    caption="Recordable injuries per 100 full-time workers, 2016 to 2024, from the annual summaries employers file with OSHA."
    columns={['Workplace', 'Rate']}
    rows={star.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="star" set:html={JSON.stringify(starSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Both of those are true at once, and neither is a criticism of the rating. The five-star formula
    combines an inspection record, a staffing figure and a set of clinical measures taken from
    resident assessments. All three are about residents. None of them is about the people who lift
    them, and nothing in a five-star badge says so.
  </p>
  <Gloss>
    Which is a rule rather than an observation about one building. A composite index arrives with an
    authority its inputs do not have — one digit, from a federal agency, on a five-point scale — and
    its scope lives in a methodology document nobody following a link will read. So a rating is
    published here only beside what it scores and at least one consequential thing at the same
    address it cannot see. Resisting the next step matters too: across the twelve homes the rating
    and the injury rate correlate −0.165 over nine years and −0.617 with the pandemic years removed,
    and the second was the sixth thing tried. Twelve homes support a sentence about two named homes
    and not a coefficient.
  </Gloss>

  <h2>A seventh smaller</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={days} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="days"
    title="Thirteen years of resident days"
    caption="Total days billed by the county's certified nursing homes. The peak year is 2013 and the trough is 2022."
    columns={['Year', 'Resident days']}
    rows={days.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="days" set:html={JSON.stringify(daysSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={mcd} />
  <p>
    Which makes the state the customer. Whatever else a nursing home in this county is, it is mostly
    a business that sells days to Medicaid, and the long residence has held up better than the short
    rehabilitation stay that Medicare buys. The staffing has fallen faster than either: the sector
    employed 1,945 people here in 2011 and 1,419 in 2023, against a fall of under a tenth in the
    days they cover.
  </p>
</Article>
